==================================================================================================
1  DOES THE SLOPE RECOVER THE HURST EXPONENT ON ONE INTRADAY WINDOW OF 72 RETURNS?
==================================================================================================
   SIMULATED random-walk benchmark at N=72:  k>=1 +0.5305   k>=3 +0.5436
   (the closed form says 0.5000 -- the finite-window bias is +0.0305, which is why
    the benchmark is simulated and not assumed)

   true Hu   slope k>=3   vs benchmark   per-session sd   sessions to resolve at 2 SE
    0.40      +0.6465      +0.1028         0.2076               17
    0.45      +0.5915      +0.0479         0.2047               74
    0.50      +0.5456      +0.0019         0.2050            44537
    0.55      +0.4928      -0.0509         0.2007               63
    0.60      +0.4432      -0.1005         0.1986               16
    0.65      +0.3954      -0.1482         0.1967                8

   Hu=0.55 -- a MILD persistent trend -- resolves in a few dozen sessions, i.e. about a
   MONTH. That is the scale a regime lives on, and it is measured entirely inside the day.

==================================================================================================
2  IS IT VOLATILITY-INVARIANT, AND DOES STANDARDISING COST THE SIGNAL?
==================================================================================================
   profile                        RAW slope   drift from ref    STANDARDISED   drift from ref
   flat vol (reference)             +0.4927     +0.0000          +0.5026       +0.0000
   vol x4 overall                   +0.4927     +0.0000          +0.5026       +0.0000
   U-shaped intraday vol            +0.4667     -0.0260          +0.5004       -0.0022
   one 10x bar mid-session          +0.4339     -0.0589          +0.4816       -0.0211
   vol ramp 1 -> 5                  +0.4946     +0.0018          +0.5024       -0.0002

   AND THE SIGNAL SURVIVES STANDARDISATION: Hu=0.55 sits -0.0509 from its benchmark
   raw and -0.0450 standardised (benchmarks +0.5436 and +0.5477 respectively).
   So vol-standardising buys profile-invariance; whether it costs sensitivity is the
   comparison above, and it must be made against the STANDARDISED benchmark, not the raw one.

==================================================================================================
3  BID-ASK BOUNCE: does dropping k=1,2 actually fix it? (MEASURED, not assumed)
==================================================================================================
   Truth is Hu=0.50 throughout. psi/sigma is the half-spread over per-bar sigma.

   psi/sigma   k>=1 slope   vs k>=1 bench   k>=3 slope   vs k>=3 bench   standardised k>=3
      0.00      +0.5283      -0.0022       +0.5390      -0.0046        -0.0044
      0.10      +0.5309      +0.0004       +0.5402      -0.0034        -0.0036
      0.25      +0.5434      +0.0129       +0.5469      +0.0033        +0.0013
      0.50      +0.5815      +0.0509       +0.5689      +0.0252        +0.0215
      1.00      +0.6773      +0.1467       +0.6386      +0.0950        +0.0853

   BOUNCE SENSITIVITY over psi/sigma 0 -> 1:  k>=1 +0.1490   k>=3 +0.0996   -> dropping k=1,2 removes 33% of it
   IT IS A MITIGATION, NOT A FIX. Against the correct benchmark a psi/sigma of 1 still reads
   as strong false mean reversion. A bounce-free price is required: the mid from the bbo-1m
   or tbbo already on disk. ZT and SR3 sit at the high-psi end of this table.

