4  ON THE REAL FIXTURE: is the PER-SESSION slope a TIME SERIES with structure?
==================================================================================================
   (the principal: a mean over all time throws out the useful part)

   Benchmarks at N=72, k>=3:  raw +0.5436   vol-standardised +0.5477
   A slope BELOW its benchmark means Hu > 0.5, i.e. PERSISTENT / trending.

   root  sessions   raw slope   vs bench   std slope   vs bench   lag-1 ac   yearly-mean sd
     NQ     1,960   +0.5280   -0.0156    +0.5395   -0.0082   -0.1019     0.0114   (n dropped 7)
     ES     1,957   +0.5259   -0.0177    +0.5378   -0.0098   -0.0564     0.0208   (n dropped 12)
     YM     1,958   +0.5261   -0.0176    +0.5382   -0.0095   -0.0121     0.0209   (n dropped 7)
    RTY     1,467   +0.5265   -0.0171    +0.5365   -0.0112   -0.0232     0.0215   (n dropped 6)
     CL     1,903   +0.5258   -0.0179    +0.5528   +0.0052   -0.0104     0.0238   (n dropped 14)
     GC     3,139   +0.5461   +0.0025    +0.5728   +0.0251   -0.0391     0.0116   (n dropped 23)
     ZN     3,098   +0.5458   +0.0021    +0.5622   +0.0145   -0.0144     0.0080   (n dropped 91)
     ZT     2,599   +0.5801   +0.0364    +0.5600   +0.0123   +0.0259     0.0417   (n dropped 283)
     6E     3,154   +0.5539   +0.0103    +0.5739   +0.0263   -0.0145     0.0163   (n dropped 23)
     NG     1,910   +0.5343   -0.0093    +0.5624   +0.0148   -0.0187     0.0140   (n dropped 10)

   THE LAST TWO COLUMNS ARE THE ANSWER TO 'STOP POOLING': a non-zero lag-1 autocorrelation
   and a yearly-mean spread say the slope is a PERSISTENT STATE with regimes -- an object a
   causal feature could predict, and precisely what a pooled mean over 84,776 sessions
   destroys. Per-session sd from section 1 is about 0.13, so compare the yearly spread to
   0.13/sqrt(sessions per year) rather than to 0.13.
